### 1. Formal Architectural Design & Concurrency Invariants

#### 1.1 Memory Model & Progression Invariants
The ring buffer topology implements a **Ticketed Slot-Sequence Bounded Multi-Producer Multi-Consumer (MPMC)** design combined with a decoupled **Epoch-Based Reclamation (EBR)** subsystem.

```
       PRODUCER BATCH LEASE                     SLOT ARRAY (Cacheline-Isolated)                   CONSUMER BATCH LEASE
  [reserve_produce_batch(N)]                                                                     [reserve_consume_batch(N)]
              │                                                                                              │
  fetch_add(N, relaxed)                                                                          fetch_add(N, relaxed)
              │                                                                                              │
              ▼                                                                                              ▼
   ┌──────────────────────┐      ┌─────────────────┬─────────────────┬─────────────────┐      ┌──────────────────────┐
   │ ProducerLease<T>     │ ───► │ Slot [k]        │ Slot [k+1]      │ Slot [k+2]      │ ◄─── │ ConsumerLease<T>     │
   │ Span: [k, k+N)       │      │ seq: (k+1)      │ seq: (k+2)      │ seq: (k+3)      │      │ Span: [k, k+N)       │
   │ Commit on Drop/RAII  │      │ payload: T      │ payload: T      │ payload: T      │      │ Commit on Drop/RAII  │
   └──────────────────────┘      └─────────────────┴─────────────────┴─────────────────┘      └──────────────────────┘
              │                           ▲                 ▲                 ▲                          │
              └────── seq.store(k+1, ────-┴─────────────────┴─────────────────┴──────── seq.store(k+Cap, ──┘
                             release)                                                              release)
```

##### 1. Sequence Invariant
For a ring buffer of capacity $C = 2^m$ and slot index $i = \text{ticket} \pmod C$:
- **Empty / Writable State**: $\text{slot}[i].\text{sequence} == \text{ticket}$
- **Committed / Readable State**: $\text{slot}[i].\text{sequence} == \text{ticket} + 1$
- **Consumed / Recycled State**: $\text{slot}[i].\text{sequence} == \text{ticket} + C$

##### 2. Happens-Before ($\to_{\text{hb}}$) Synchronization Chains
- **Producer $\to$ Consumer Synchronization**:
  $$\text{Producer Lease Write} \to_{\text{po}} \text{slot}[i].\text{sequence}.\text{store}(\text{ticket} + 1, \text{release})$$
  $$\text{slot}[i].\text{sequence}.\text{load}(\text{acquire}) == \text{ticket} + 1 \to_{\text{po}} \text{Consumer Lease Read}$$
  Because $\text{store}(\text{release})$ synchronizes with $\text{load}(\text{acquire})$, all memory modifications to slot storage performed during the `ProducerLease` are visible to the consumer prior to reading the slot payload.

- **Consumer $\to$ Next Producer Synchronization**:
  $$\text{Consumer Read/Drop} \to_{\text{po}} \text{slot}[i].\text{sequence}.\text{store}(\text{ticket} + C, \text{release})$$
  $$\text{slot}[i].\text{sequence}.\text{load}(\text{acquire}) == \text{ticket} + C \to_{\text{po}} \text{Next Producer Overwrite}$$
  This prevents the next producer lap from overwriting slot storage while an active consumer is still reading.

##### 3. Out-of-Order Commit Non-Blocking Invariant
When batch reservations interleave (e.g., Producer $A$ reserves $[k, k+4)$ and Producer $B$ reserves $[k+4, k+8)$), Producer $B$ can write and commit its batch before Producer $A$. Consumers attempting to read $[k+4, k+8)$ will observe valid slot sequences and proceed independently, eliminating Head-of-Line (HoL) blocking at the slot level.

##### 4. Epoch-Based Reclamation (EBR) Tri-Epoch Invariant
The global epoch $G \in \{0, 1, 2\}$ advances when all registered threads are observed to have quiescent states or active epochs $e_i \in \{G, (G-1) \bmod 3\}$. Any object retired in epoch $e_{\text{retire}}$ is guaranteed to have no concurrent active readers when $G = (e_{\text{retire}} + 2) \bmod 3$, allowing deferred batch deallocation without atomic reference counting.

